## When a word reads wrong

Skribisto is translated by people who write in your language. If a menu, a message or a
help page reads wrong to you, tell them. Choose *Help ▸ Report a Translation Error…*.

You can also start from these places:

- *Report a translation error* in the *Learn* page of the window Skribisto opens on.
- *Report a translation error* under the language choice, in *Settings ▸ Appearance*.
- *Report a translation error on this page*, at the foot of any help page shown in your
  language. The search then lists only that page.

The report window stays open beside your work. Go and look at the menu again if you need
to, then come back.

## Find the text

The report is most useful when it names the exact line to change. So rather than
describing where you saw the text, you find it.

- Check the *Language*. It follows the language of the interface until you pick another.
- Type a few words of the text in *Find the text*. Accents and capitals do not matter.
- Pick the text in the list. Below each one you see where it appears and the English it
  translates.

If the same words appear in several places, the list shows them once and says how many
places use them. *Which place?* then lets you choose one, or all of them. Choose all of
them when the problem is a word used everywhere, such as a term your language says
differently.

You can also type the English text, if you know it. The list then shows what your
language says for it.

## When the search finds nothing

Press *I can’t find it* and say where you saw the text. Some words do not come from
Skribisto at all. The file dialogs, for example, belong to your operating system.

## Say what is wrong

- Under *What is wrong?*, pick the closest kind of problem.
- Under *Explain (optional, in any language)*, say why it reads wrong.
- Under *Better translation*, write how it should read. The box starts with the text as it
  is now, so you only change what is wrong.

Leave the special marks in place:

- The `&` in a menu name marks the letter used to reach it from the keyboard. Keep one.
- A part in braces, such as `{ $count }`, is where Skribisto puts a number or a name.
- A text that changes with a number lists each form. Correct every one.

Some names are written into your project when you create it: the first statuses, the
preset tags, the note templates. A correction changes them for new projects only. You can
rename them in yours yourself.

## Send it

Press *Continue on GitHub*. Your browser opens a public report on the project’s GitHub
page, already filled in. Check it, tick the box about the Contributor License Agreement,
and press *Create*.

You need a free GitHub account, and the page is in English. *What will be public* shows
exactly what the report says before you send it. It holds no project names and none of
your writing.

A long report, or one in a script such as Japanese or Arabic, may not fit in the link.
Skribisto then copies the whole report for you, and the fields that could not travel say
to paste it.

## If something goes wrong

- If your browser did not open, press *Copy link* and paste the link into your browser.
- If GitHub shows an error page or loses your report, press *Copy text*. Open a new
  translation report on GitHub and paste it.
- *Copy text* also suits other channels, such as the project’s Discord.

Press *Report another* to start again in the same language.
